  ],
  "result": {
    "architecture_recommendations": [
      {
        "recommendation": "Use Random Forest with engineered features as the baseline (Model A), not XGBoost. Benchmark everything against it before investing in deep learning.",
        "rationale": "The Swiss SLF operational system (Perez-Guillen et al. 2022, 2025) uses Random Forest and achieves ~70% agreement with human-published danger levels. RAvaFcast v1.0.0 (Maissen et al. 2024) scales this to regional maps. RF is the only architecture deployed operationally in avalanche forecasting worldwide. XGBoost has not been shown to outperform RF in this domain, while RF provides better interpretability via feature importance -- critical for safety-critical forecaster trust. The NHESS 2023 neural network study showed NNs achieve comparable (76.54% validation) but not superior performance to RF, confirming tree-based methods as the right baseline.",
        "evidence": "ML architectures dimension (Swiss SLF operational RF, RAvaFcast pipeline, NHESS 2023 NN comparison); Physics dimension (SNOWPACK+RF hybrid as state-of-the-art)"
      },
      {
        "recommendation": "Implement a two-stage classification pipeline: Stage 1 predicts avalanche problem types (Persistent Slab, Storm Slab, Loose Wet), Stage 2 predicts danger level using problem-type predictions as intermediate features alongside weather and derived snowpack features.",
        "rationale": "Schwartzreich and Rodriguez (2026) demonstrated this exact architecture on 28,140 region-band-days across 8 CAIC forecast regions in Colorado. This is the single most directly relevant paper -- it works on Colorado data, with CAIC labels, using publicly available meteorological sources. The two-stage decomposition mirrors how human forecasters reason (identify the problem type, then assess severity) and decomposes an otherwise noisy 5-class problem into tractable sub-problems. Critically, it works without the dense instrumentation (100+ stations, SNOWPACK simulations) the Swiss system requires.",
        "evidence": "ML architectures dimension (Schwartzreich & Rodriguez 2026 Colorado two-stage framework); Spatial downscaling dimension (two-stage architecture validated by RAvaFcast and Colorado-specific research)"
      },
      {
        "recommendation": "For Model B (sequence model), use LSTM with 180-day lookback as the starting architecture, not Transformer or Mamba. Test sensitivity up to 365 days.",
        "rationale": "The HESS 2025 benchmark ('From RNNs to Transformers') shows LSTM outperforms Transformers for hydrologic regression tasks with median KGE advantage of 0.11 on global streamflow. Multiple snow-hydrology papers use 365-day lookback as standard for snow-influenced catchments. LSTM cell states have been shown to naturally encode snow accumulation processes (ASCE paper on Rio Hondo basin). Transformers add value for spatial generalization, not temporal modeling at a single site. At 180 tokens (daily steps), quadratic attention cost is not a bottleneck, but the inductive bias of LSTM recurrence is better suited to accumulation-type state tracking.",
        "evidence": "Time-series architectures dimension (HESS 2025 LSTM vs Transformer benchmark, 365-day lookback standard, LSTM cell state encoding snow accumulation); Physics dimension (path-dependent weak layer evolution requiring state tracking)"
      },
      {
        "recommendation": "Implement hierarchical temporal input using N-HiTS-style multi-rate MaxPool sampling: one branch consumes the last 14-30 days at hourly or sub-daily resolution, another branch consumes the full 180-day season history at daily resolution with progressively compressed older data.",
        "rationale": "The hierarchical recent-detail plus compressed-history approach is validated by four independent lines of research: N-HiTS multi-rate sampling (AAAI 2023, outperformed LSTM for soil moisture), SST Mamba-Transformer decomposition (showed Mamba excels at long-range, Transformer at short-range), TimeMixer multiscale mixing, and dilated TCN pyramids. N-HiTS is the simplest implementation path -- it uses MaxPool at different kernel sizes so one block sees every timestep while another sees every 8th or 16th. This maps directly to snowpack physics: recent weather drives current loading and surface instability, while season history captures persistent weak layer formation from October onward.",
        "evidence": "Time-series architectures dimension (N-HiTS AAAI 2023, SST hybrid decomposition, TimeMixer, dilated TCN pyramids); Physics dimension (early-season faceting requires full-season context, recent loading drives current instability)"
      },
      {
        "recommendation": "Engineer these specific physics-informed features from weather station data, ordered by expected predictive value: (1) multi-window new snow sums (24h, 48h, 72h, 120h), (2) cumulative temperature-gradient-days proxy (estimated from air temp, snow depth, assumed 0C ground temp), (3) surface hoar formation potential index (clear-sky hours multiplied by humidity when wind is below threshold), (4) snow depth relative to seasonal climatological normal, (5) wind-weighted precipitation for wind slab loading, (6) rain-on-snow binary detection, (7) temperature trend and proximity to 0C.",
        "rationale": "Perez-Guillen et al. (2022) used 67 features combining meteorological variables and SNOWPACK-derived features. Eastern Canada studies derived 88 variables from basic meteorological data using cumulative windows of 24-120h. Snow depth changes and new snow amounts were consistently top predictors. The temperature gradient threshold of ~10-20 K/m for faceting onset is the key physics signal for persistent weak layers. These features approximate what SNOWPACK computes explicitly (stability indices, grain metamorphism) without requiring the full physics simulation infrastructure. The 2026 Colorado paper confirmed these derived features work for CAIC danger prediction.",
        "evidence": "Physics dimension (SNOWPACK feature importance, metamorphism thresholds, weak layer formation physics, simplified proxy features); ML architectures dimension (67-88 engineered features in successful systems, Schwartzreich 2026 Colorado derived features)"
      },
      {
        "recommendation": "Target binary or 3-class danger output (e.g., Low/Moderate vs Considerable/High/Extreme) as the primary prediction, with probability distributions over all 5 levels as secondary output. Do not optimize for 5-class accuracy.",
        "rationale": "The Northern Norway study (Cryosphere 2025) showed binary classification achieves 76% accuracy versus only 57% for 4-level prediction. Danger levels 4-5 are too rare for reliable prediction. The Swiss SLF operational system outputs probability distributions that forecasters interpret, not hard classifications. Binary elevated-vs-not-elevated is the most actionable output for safety decisions. The class imbalance problem (avalanche events are ~1.1% of days) compounds the multi-class difficulty.",
        "evidence": "ML architectures dimension (Northern Norway 76% binary vs 57% 4-class, extreme class imbalance at 1.1%, SLF probability distribution output); Data sources dimension (CAIC danger ratings as labels)"
      },
      {
        "recommendation": "For Phase 2 spatial downscaling, use HRRR 3km grids as primary input (not SNOTEL interpolation), bias-corrected against SNOTEL observations, with XGBoost over DEM-derived terrain features (elevation, TPI at 500m, slope, aspect, sky view factor, curvature) as the downscaling model. Add explicit cold-air-pool handling using topographic position metrics.",
        "rationale": "SNOTEL stations are 20-50km apart in Colorado -- too sparse for sub-km interpolation (Fassnacht et al. 2003). HRRR provides hourly 3km coverage but has 25-65% precipitation bias in Colorado mountains (NOAA evaluation). The fix is HRRR as base layer with SNOTEL bias correction. A 2024 Alps intercomparison found CNN outperforms RF for temperature downscaling, but XGBoost with terrain features is simpler and well-validated as a starting point. Cold air pools and inversions are the hardest sub-problem -- a tiered ML approach with TPI and catchment geometry reduces valley RMSE by 50-70% (2025 interpretable ML paper). TopoPyScale provides a ready-made physically-based baseline for comparison.",
        "evidence": "Spatial downscaling dimension (SNOTEL spacing limitations, HRRR bias evaluation, cold air pool ML correction, TPI importance, TopoPyScale baseline); Data sources dimension (SNOTEL 118 stations, HRRR on AWS)"
      },
      {
        "recommendation": "Design the system as human-in-the-loop decision support from day one, not as an autonomous forecaster. Output explanations (SHAP values, feature contributions) alongside every prediction.",
        "rationale": "Even the most advanced operational system worldwide (Swiss SLF, running since 2021) positions ML as a 'second opinion' at the forecaster briefing table. The SLF emphasizes human experts will continue to produce the bulletin. SHAP values from tree-based models are a significant practical advantage -- forecasters can see that the model flagged danger because of 72h new snow accumulation plus a persistent weak layer signal, not just a number. This is not just a nice-to-have; it is a prerequisite for adoption in safety-critical avalanche forecasting.",
        "evidence": "ML architectures dimension (SLF operational deployment as second opinion since 2021, explainability as first-class requirement); Physics dimension (forecaster trust requires understanding model reasoning)"
      }
    ],
    "temporal_window": {
      "recommendation": "Use a 180-day lookback starting from October 1 (season start) with hierarchical multi-rate sampling: full hourly resolution for the most recent 7 days, daily resolution for the last 30 days, and 3-day aggregated resolution for the remainder of the season back to October 1. Test sensitivity up to 365 days. The 30-day-only window proposed earlier is confirmed as dangerously insufficient.",
      "rationale": "Early season conditions (October-December) determine avalanche hazard for the entire winter. The 2022-23 deep persistent slab problem in western Canada was directly caused by below-average November-December snowpack. Basal facets formed in thin early-season snowpacks persist as the primary avalanche problem through the entire season. A model looking at only the last 30 days would completely miss deep persistent slab problems, which are the most dangerous and hardest-to-predict type. The hydrology literature standardizes on 365-day lookback for snow-influenced catchments (multiple LSTM papers). N-HiTS multi-rate sampling (AAAI 2023) validates that different model blocks can consume input at different temporal resolutions. The SST paper (2024) confirms that decomposing long-range and short-range signals and routing each to an appropriate model (Mamba for long, Transformer/LSTM for short) outperforms either alone. Starting at 180 days captures a full accumulation season while keeping computational cost reasonable.",
      "academic_precedent": "365-day lookback is standard in snow-hydrology LSTM models (HESS 2022, ASCE snowmelt papers). N-HiTS (AAAI 2023) validated hierarchical multi-rate temporal sampling outperforming flat LSTM for soil moisture. Perez-Guillen et al. (2022) use 24h averaging windows within multi-day cumulative features (24h, 48h, 72h, 96h, 120h). The 2022-23 DPS analysis from Avalanche Canada documents season-spanning weak layer persistence. Eastern Canada studies derive 88 variables using cumulative windows up to 120h but do not model full-season context, which is identified as a gap."
    },
    "model_choices": {
      "recommended_baseline": "Random Forest with 67-88 engineered features following the Perez-Guillen et al. (2022) feature design. Use multi-window cumulative weather features (24h through 120h sums/averages for precipitation, temperature, wind) plus the physics-informed proxy features (temperature gradient days, surface hoar potential index, snow depth anomaly, wind loading index, rain-on-snow flag). Implement the Schwartzreich & Rodriguez (2026) two-stage architecture: Stage 1 RF predicts avalanche problem types (Persistent Slab, Storm Slab, Loose Wet), Stage 2 RF uses those predictions as features alongside weather features to predict danger level per elevation band. This is directly validated on Colorado CAIC data across 8 forecast regions.",
      "recommended_sequence": "LSTM with 180-day lookback using N-HiTS-style multi-rate input sampling. Architecture: parallel branches with MaxPool at kernel sizes 1 (recent 14 days at daily resolution), 7 (last 60 days compressed), and 14 (full 180-day season compressed). Each branch feeds a separate LSTM, outputs concatenated for prediction. This is simpler to implement than custom Mamba-Transformer hybrids and validated by N-HiTS (AAAI 2023) outperforming flat LSTM for geophysical prediction. If this underperforms, upgrade the long-range branch to Mamba (linear complexity, validated for 120-hour streamflow forecasting on EarthArXiv 2025) while keeping LSTM for the recent-detail branch. Do NOT use vanilla Transformer -- HESS 2025 benchmark shows LSTM outperforms Transformers for hydrologic regression by KGE 0.11.",
      "physics_informed_approach": "Two-tier architecture without running SNOWPACK: (1) Physics feature engineering layer computes proxy features from raw weather data -- cumulative temperature gradient days (air_temp - 0C ground / snow_depth, accumulated when gradient exceeds 10 K/m threshold), surface hoar formation index (hours where clear-sky fraction > 0.8 AND relative humidity > 80% AND wind speed < 2 m/s), new snow loading at 5 time windows (24h/48h/72h/96h/120h), wind transport index (wind_speed * sin(wind_direction - slope_aspect) * precipitation), rain-on-snow events (precipitation when air_temp > 0C with existing snowpack), snow depth relative to 30-year SNOTEL climatological mean (thin snowpack = faceting risk). (2) These features plus raw weather time series feed both the RF baseline and sequence model. This approximates what SNOWPACK computes explicitly (Viallon-Galinier et al. 2023 confirmed physics-based stability indices outperform meteorological features alone) without requiring the simulation infrastructure. If accuracy plateaus, consider integrating SNOWPACK simulations at SNOTEL station locations as a Phase 1.5 enhancement -- the RAvaFcast pipeline validates this exact upgrade path."
    },
    "data_sources": [
